You are screening the last day of literature for a researcher who already knows these search terms to an extent: $terms.

The papers below come from a PubMed topic search across all of those terms. Decide which papers are worth the researcher's time.

Rules — keep primary research articles relevant and significant to this field:
- Significant means a novel or field-advancing contribution — e.g. a new variant of the field's central tool, a new delivery method, or a finding that overturns an accepted assumption.
- A mechanism from another system is relevant only if the paper states it transfers to the system you are screening for; a model result is relevant if a practical tool could be engineered from it. A topic may not appear relevant at first, like a new analysis pipeline, but actually it may be such as using it to identify new disease-associated loci or new candidate targets.
- Work on a delivery route can be relevant, but a route used solely for vaccines is likely not.
- Search terms may bring up irrelevant papers: a well-known tool used merely as a method for an unrelated question is not relevant.
- Judge novelty relative to the field, in context. Do not be swayed by buzzwords: applying a standard method to a new subject is not novel per se, since many subjects have been tested before; it is novel only if it is a clinical trial, a new mechanism, subject-agnostic, or similarly field-advancing.
- Exclude suspected paper mills (suspicious authors, tortured phrases, low-reputation journals, etc.).

Pick only the most relevant and significant papers. Diversity is key, if two papers pioneered a new method that is very similar in process or aim, then only one needs to be picked.

Output: a single JSON object and nothing else, for example:

{"selected": [3, 7, 12]}

List every paper number you are keeping, in the order they appear in the list below.

Papers:

$body
